course: dictionary
lesson: 4710
title: Words that show respect
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Honourable is an adjective. It describes someone who is good, honest and fair, and deserves respect. The words around it are its context. «She lost the race but played with an honourable spirit» would suggest she lost well, not that she cheated or sulked.
explain: In sport, an «honourable defeat» is a loss where you still played fairly and well. An «honourable draw» is a result where neither side cheated to get it. Both phrases show that honourable is about fairness, not about winning.
explain: Honourable is also a formal title. In Parliament, a politician may call a colleague «my honourable friend», and some judges and officials are called «the Honourable». The capital letter and the grown-up setting are your clues that this is a title.
explain: The Chinese example uses the same idea of respect. Grown-ups ask «nín guì xìng?» («neen GWAY SHING»), which means «your honourable surname?». Guì is the word for honourable, and it is used to show respect to the other person. Read the brackets and the context together to work out the meaning.
fact: Honourable means good, honest and fair, and deserving respect.
fact: «An honourable defeat» means you lost but played fairly and well.
fact: «The Honourable» is a formal title used for some judges, politicians and officials.
fact: In Chinese, «guì» (honourable) shows respect to the other person, as in «nín guì xìng?» (your honourable surname?).
fact: Spelling: honourable is British English; the US spelling is honorable, with the same meaning.
exercise: What does honourable mean in «an honourable draw»? | *Done fairly, so you keep your honour even without winning | Very expensive | A draw that happens in the rain | Easy to win
exercise: In the Chinese example, what does guì mean? | Little | *Honourable, used to show respect to the other person | Surname | Old friend
exercise: In Parliament, a politician may call another «my honourable friend». Why? | To say they are a close relative | To say they are older | *It is a polite title that shows respect | To say they are lost
test: In a story, a hero who keeps promises and tells the truth is described as honourable. Which word is the best synonym? | Rude | *Honest | Loud | Tired
test: What does «honourable mention» mean? | A late entry | A rude comment | *A prize for doing well without winning | A prize for winning first place
exam: Why is «guì» used in «nín guì xìng?» (your honourable surname?) | It means goodbye | It means your name | *It shows respect to the person you are speaking to | It shows you are angry
